#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106853} :activity/id #uuid "21a47620-e3ce-58d1-b236-202e42fddc8e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049388 :topic/id #uuid "f0cae3f1-329e-50d8-bc2d-f933c5aca17d" :topic/math-academy-id 1397} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106853 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107053 :tutorial/content "The **area of a triangle** can be computed using the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac{b \\cdot h}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $b$ is a *base* of the triangle and $h$ is the corresponding *height*.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's compute the area of the triangle with base \n$b = 4$\nand the corresponding height is\n$h = 8,$\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/84/84de11aac8e9f33a459f2a83647dac43eb2054445d842ac769511711366a15ec.png)\n\nThe area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 8}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{2} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** At the end of the lesson, we'll demonstrate where the formula comes from. But first, let's get some practice using it." :tutorial/id #uuid "bf61df49-32c4-50ee-a6f4-21f9da391a02" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2306 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4293d690-2bd3-5e64-91c6-7e02a0c764f9" :step/math-academy-id 416085 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106854}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106854 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105870 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105541 :question/id #uuid "fc5fc31e-f585-5d15-8a8a-eb0d19704e9a" :question/math-academy-id "e-2947"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6c58537-344e-5842-b68c-d9b5a9d7ca96" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048472} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049608}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508973}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of a Triangle"} :step/id #uuid "a5cb1ab1-345e-5828-babf-f43dc62b43f0" :step/math-academy-id 416086 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106855}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106855 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105871 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105542 :question/id #uuid "cdb1d145-6e8a-5213-a7f7-8076440cbbeb" :question/math-academy-id "e-2949"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6445834c-7788-5427-a72c-30b06cc8e6ab" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050428}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354594} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354604} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354605} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399961}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of an Isosceles Right Triangle"} :step/id #uuid "bf4e1141-e78b-57d6-8b32-737373cca813" :step/math-academy-id 416089 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106856}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106856 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105872 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105543 :question/id #uuid "58e1ce31-f6fc-5900-ab89-e00241045b12" :question/math-academy-id "e-2950"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7656d23-06c9-5d22-b62a-3636bca66ab6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508996}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Base or the Height of a Triangle Given Its Area"} :step/id #uuid "e81bf9a1-61c3-5dc2-8537-01fb6d1a7871" :step/math-academy-id 416092 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106857}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106857 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107055 :tutorial/content "We know that the area formula for a triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac{b \\cdot h}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $b$ is a base and $h$ is the corresponding height.\n\nBut where does this formula come from?\n\nTo understand where the formula comes from, consider $\\triangle{ABC}$ below.\n\n![](images/02/02c1ace5f18e9204c1559f08f591ecd692f00691c48cbee81bc4426c3b34400e.png)\n\nLet's draw a rectangle around $\\triangle{ABC},$ as follows:\n\n![](images/89/893026324410235de9cb7ac286867006593a451556587c60219fe4375a4218a4.png)\n\nWe have two pairs of right triangles with equal areas (each pair is indicated with arrows).\n\nSo, the area of $\\triangle{ABC}$ is exactly half of the area of the rectangle. This gives\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}_{ABC} = \\dfrac{AB \\cdot CH}{2}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b5be18d9-4833-50c8-8800-085d64e21ce8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2311 :tutorial/title "Proof of the Formula"} :step/id #uuid "d47b1a6f-6091-5f5b-832a-9ce0b04d1526" :step/math-academy-id 416095}] :activity/title "Areas of Triangles" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186106016}]}
